WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.850
John Franklin
Sir John Franklin was knighted for his first

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:05.950
two journeys of exploration to the Arctic.
Yet, his third trip to the ice would be prove

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.420
to be one of the most disastrous sea-borne
expeditions of all time. He and his 128 men

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.030
simply vanished. For nearly a decade a massive
search was undertaken. In the end though,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:21.769
it was the perseverance of a wife who refused
to let go that uncovered the crew’s terrible

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:26.720
fate. In today’s Biographics, we discover
the real story of Sir John Franklin and his

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.880
voyage of terror.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:43.210
Early Years
John Franklin was born on April 16th, 1786

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.110
in Spilsby, Lincolnshire in England. He was
the ninth of twelve children to Willingham

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.230
Franklin and Hannah Weekes. Willingham was
a successful merchant, which enabled him to

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.080
provide a comfortable standard of living for
his family.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.920
John attended school at King Edward VI Grammar
School. He got his first view of the ocean

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.671
at the age of ten and it was at that point
that he set his mind on a career at sea. His

00:01:02.671 --> 00:01:06.620
father was not happy with the decision as
he had a career in the church in mind for

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:11.280
John. Within two years, however, Willingham
had softened. He allowed John to experience

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.310
life at sea by going on a trial voyage on
a merchant ship. This experience confirmed

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.970
for John that this was the life for him.
He joined the Royal Navy at age 14. Within

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.270
a few months he got his first taste of naval
warfare at the Battle of Copenhagen.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:28.031
At the tender age of fifteen, Franklin served
as a midshipman on HMS Investigator. This

00:01:28.031 --> 00:01:32.720
ship was captained by Matthew Flinders with
the mission to chart the Australian coast.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.560
Flinders was a cousin to John and he took
a personal interest in the training of the

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.030
eager teenager. Over the course of the two-year
expedition, he was hands on in helping the

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.670
boy get to grips with the fundamentals of
navigation and surveying.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.880
After two years, the Investigator became unseaworthy,
forcing the crew to transfer to the HMS Porpoise

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.240
for the home journey. This ship was wrecked
on a reef 200 miles north of Australia. Captain

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.100
Flinders set off with several officers in
an open boat en route to Sydney for help.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.890
Franklin and the remainder of the crew were
left to camp on the narrow reef, using strips

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:08.450
of sail for makeshift tents. Six weeks later,
Flinders was back with a rescue ship.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.370
Upon his return from Australia, Franklin secured
a position as a signals officer on HMS Bellerophon.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.410
He was in the middle of fighting at the Battle
of Trafalgar in 1805. He did not suffer any

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.590
wounds but the roar of cannon fire left him
slightly deaf for the rest of his life.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.610
Seven years later, the now very experienced
twenty-six-year-old was involved in the Battle

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:30.750
of New Orleans during the War of 1812. He
was at the head of a party that tried, but

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.070
failed, to capture New Orleans.
By the time of the end of war against the

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:39.120
United States, Franklin had risen to the rank
of lieutenant. But, seeing that it was a time

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.420
of peace, there were now fewer opportunities
for promotion. For three years, Franklin struggled

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:48.540
to secure appointments. Then, in 1819, at
the age of 32, he was given the mission of

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.850
heading an expedition to the Arctic. It was
his first command.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:59.780
To the Arctic
The two ships that were to be used for the

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.850
Arctic expedition were not naval vessels.
Rather, they were whaling ships that had been

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.870
purchased by the navy. The ship that Franklin
was to lead the expedition from was the Trent.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.020
The other ship was the Dorothea, with a Captain
Buchan on board. The mission was to head north

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.360
in an attempt to reach the North Pole and
then take a direct line to the Bering Strait

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.930
in order to finds a Northwest Passage.
The mission was predicated on the Open Polar

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.560
Sea theory. The theory was that the sea temperature
increased between the Arctic Circle and the

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.590
North Pole. By this reasoning, once a ship
had broken through the ice sheet north of

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.270
Spitsbergen, they would be able to make it
through. Prior to the voyage, meetings were

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:38.560
held in which scientists explained to Franklin
and Buchan the rationale behind the theory.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.920
Acting on this information, Franklin set himself
to making observations on the tides and currents,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:48.110
the depth of the sea, terrestrial magnetism
ahd atmospheric goings on during the voyage.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:52.500
The ships set off from Shetland. Prior to
departure the Trent was letting in water.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.310
The source of the leak could not be located.
It required the men to constantly work the

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:01.129
pumps. Franklin tried to recruit sailors from
Shetland but many of them refused, stating

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:06.730
that they would go to sea on a leaking ship.
This was not a good start. It meant that Franklin

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:11.510
set off short-handed and with a vessel that
he could not have full trust in. The men had

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.710
to man the pump and work the windlass at the
same time. This put an inordinate amount of

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:20.459
pressure on the crew and some of them became
overtaken with exhaustion. It took several

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.520
weeks until the leak was finally discovered
and repaired.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.059
In the winter of 1819, Franklin set of on
his first overland expedition in the North-West

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:32.080
territoriy of Canada. The party stopped off
at Cumberland House, which was a fur trading

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.870
depot on the Saskatchewan River. The supplies
that they were expecting to find were not

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:41.279
there. A thousand miles further on at Fort
Chipewyan they reached another supply station,

00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:45.379
but again there were no supplies.
Rather than turn back, Franklin carried on,

00:04:45.379 --> 00:04:49.499
though he had only a single day’s provisions.
He told the men that they would hunt and fish

00:04:49.499 --> 00:04:55.089
to feed themselves. This was a forlorn hope
and the men very nearly starved to death.

00:04:55.089 --> 00:04:59.490
The expedition eventually made it to the Arctic
coast. They were, however, only able to explore

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:03.840
500 miles before the lack of food and harsh
winter forced them to turn back.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.680
An overland journey across the so-called Barren
lands was an unmitigated disaster. The party

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:13.460
of twenty-two men stumbled through uncharted
territory in a state of near starvation. For

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:18.559
days on end, all they had to eat was lichen.
Eleven men died, with the others being rescued

00:05:18.559 --> 00:05:22.770
by Copper Indians who helped them get to the
safety of Fort Enterprise.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:26.999
Back in England, Franklin was given a hero’s
reception. He was lauded for the courage he

00:05:26.999 --> 00:05:31.930
had displayed during adversity, becoming known
as the man who ate his boots for his resorting

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:35.110
to chewing on his boot leather while in a
state of starvation.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.909
After recovering from the ordeal of his first
command, Franklin met and fell in love with

00:05:38.909 --> 00:05:44.749
a girl by the name of Eleanor Anne Porden.
In 1824, they had a daughter, Eleanor Isabella.

00:05:44.749 --> 00:05:49.659
In February, 1825 Franklin set out another
overland journey. This time he planned to

00:05:49.659 --> 00:05:53.569
follow the course of the Mackenzie River to
the sea and then explore the coast. A week

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:58.819
after he left his wife Eleanor died of tuberculosis.
Franklin had no knowledge of the tragedy and

00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:03.680
faithfully wrote letters to his wife until
April, at which time he read of her death

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.879
in a newspaper.
Despite the personal tragedy, Franklin’s

00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:10.659
second voyage was far more successful than
his first. He returned to England in 1827

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:14.939
to much acclaim. The following year he remarried.
His second wife’s name was Jane Griffin,

00:06:14.939 --> 00:06:19.490
who had been a friend of his first wife.
Franklin was knighted in 1829 to recognize

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:23.960
his arctic exploration work. During these
years on land he put on a substantial amount

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.319
of weight, perhaps in compensation for his
years of starvation on the ice. From 1830

00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:32.379
until 1833, he commanded the HMS Rainbow as
it patrolled the Mediterranean during the

00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:36.849
Greek War of Independence. Then, in 1836,
he was made lieutenant governor of Van Diemen’s

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:40.349
Land in Australia. Today this is known as
Tasmania.

00:06:40.349 --> 00:06:44.479
Franklin enthusiastically threw himself into
the task of reforming the island. But he was

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:49.689
stifled by colonial infighting. His colonial
secretary, John Montagu seemed to undermine

00:06:49.689 --> 00:06:53.999
every move that he made. Eventually he sacked
the man. Montague complained to the British

00:06:53.999 --> 00:06:57.509
Prime Minister who agreed that the dismissal
had been unlawful. The decision was made to

00:06:57.509 --> 00:07:01.460
recall Franklin. However, the situation was
handled badly with the replacement lieutenant

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:06.330
governor arriving in Tasmania four days before
Franklin had even been informed of his dismissal.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:11.830
A deeply frustrated Franklin arrived back
in London in June, 1844. The timing was fortuitous

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:15.900
as the British Admiralty was planning another
Arctic expedition. From the moment he heard

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:20.039
about it, Franklin was desperate to lead it.
He felt that he was extremely qualified for

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:24.840
the job on the basis of his two previous expeditions.
The Admiralty agreed and Franklin was officially

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.029
appointed to command the two ships that were
to undertake the mission. They were called

00:07:29.029 --> 00:07:33.219
the Erebus and the Terror.
On the strength of Franklin’s previous Arctic

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:37.240
expeditions, only about 500 kilometers of
Arctic coastline remained unexplored. This

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:41.409
latest voyage was designed to complete the
task. The command of the Erebus was given

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:46.250
to Captain James Fitz James. Command of the
Terror was given to Captain Francis Crozier,

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:51.429
who had commanded they ship on a previous
voyage to the Antarctic between 1841 and 1844.

00:07:51.429 --> 00:07:54.529
The 59 year-old Franklin was given overall
command.

00:07:54.529 --> 00:07:58.770
Both ships had been recently outfitted with
new technology. The innovations included steam

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:03.629
engines, a library boasting more than a thousand
books and three years’ worth of preserved

00:08:03.629 --> 00:08:07.270
and tinned food supplies.
The expedition set sail from Greenhite in

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:13.789
England on May 19, 1845. In total there were
24 officers and 110 men. They headed north,

00:08:13.789 --> 00:08:18.059
picking up supplies on the Orkney Islands.
They then set a course for Greenland. They

00:08:18.059 --> 00:08:22.759
were escorted by the HMS Ratttler and a transport
ship named the Barretto Junior. Next stop

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:27.919
was Disko Island, in Baffin Bay off the west
coast of Greenland. Here five men were sent

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:31.650
home on the support ships due to illness.
This brought the final crew number down to

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.630
129.
The two ships and the men onboard were last

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:39.630
seen by European’s on July 26, 1845. On
that day, the whaler Prince of Wales came

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:44.260
across the two ships alongside an iceberg
in Lancaster Sound, between Devon and Baffin

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:47.990
Islands.
What happened after that is a matter of speculation.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:53.990
It is thought that the winter of 1845-46 was
spent on Beechey Island in Nunavut, Canada.

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:58.720
The two ships became stuck in the ice just
off King William Island some time in September,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.410
1846. They never got free.
The Search Begins

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:11.090
By early 1847, Franklin’s wife was growing
increasingly concerned at the fate of her

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:15.050
husband. Two summers had come and gone, which
was the length of time that Franklin had told

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:18.641
her he would be away. A naval officer offered
to lead an expedition to search for the two

00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:21.490
ships but the admiralty replied that they
had

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:25.210
‘unlimited confidence in the skills and
resources of Sir John Franklin’.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:29.990
As a concession, however, the admiralty did
offer a reward to any whalers who could report

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:34.070
any information about the progress of the
expedition through Lancaster Sound.

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:37.930
Meanwhile an old friend of Franklin’s by
the name of John Richardson made his own plans

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:41.620
to mount a search party. He knew that the
expedition’s food resources would run out

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:45.870
in the summer of 1848. He planned an overland
journey to the sea between the Coppermine

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:49.940
and Mackenzie rivers. This was in the case
that Franklin had made his way down the North

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:54.190
American coast. Teaming up with a group of
men from the Hudson’s Bay Company, Richardson

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:59.830
headed down the Mackenzie River to Coronation
Gulf in the summer of 1848. They found nothing.

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:03.300
Back in England, the Admiralty was starting
to get worried. With no news of the Erebus

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:08.880
and Terror by the autumn of 1847, they prepared
to send out their own search parties. In January,

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:14.300
1848, two ships set out – the HMS Plover
under a Captain Moore and the HMD Herald under

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:18.450
a Captain Kellett. They headed for the Bering
Strait to explore whether or not the two ships

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:22.900
had become stuck near the western exit to
the Strait. Two more ships, the Enterprise,

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:27.190
under Captain John Ross’ headship and the
HMS Investigator under Captain Bird headed

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:33.060
out in June, 1848 to go into Lancaster Sound
from the east in an attempt to follow Franklin’s

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.100
tracks.
These moves were not enough for Jane Franklin.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:40.220
She wrote letters to the US President and
the Russian Czar asking them to help in the

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:45.100
search operation. She also set a larger reward
for whalers than had the Admiralty. She even

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:49.040
made plans to buy her own ship and commission
her own expedition.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:52.910
Hope was raised when the captain of a whaling
ship reported that the Inuit Indians had spoken

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:57.720
of four ships that were frozen in the ice
in a channel south of Lancaster Sound. But

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.980
when the Investigator and Enterprise returned
having seen nothing in that area, the claim

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.051
was dismissed.
Further searches were sent out by the Admiralty

00:11:05.051 --> 00:11:10.240
in 1850. The HMS Investigator and the HMS
Enterprise set out again, this time to search

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:15.470
both ends of the Northwest Passage. They joined
the Plover and Herald which were still searching.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:19.450
Four more naval ships were sent to Lancaster
Sound. The ships had balloons onboard that

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:23.360
were let loose over the ice with notes attached
to tell the missing men, if they were still

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:28.720
alive, that help was on its way. Bu this time
the crew would have sent five winters on the

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:34.050
ice, so the belief that any of them were still
alive was optimistic to say the least.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.660
Private expeditions were also sent out to
join in the search. A number of ships came

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:41.600
together at the entrance to Wellington Channel
between Cornwallis and Devon Islands. Finally,

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.880
at a distance of about 230 miles from the
entrance to Lancaster Sound from Baffin Bay

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.110
they found something. At Cape Riley on Beechey
Island the remains of the 1845-46 winter camp

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:54.850
was found, along with the graves of three
men.

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:59.360
Two American ships, Advance and Rescue, joined
the search in 1850. They both got trapped

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:04.320
in ice and drifted for more than a thousand
miles out of Lancaster Sound, finally breaking

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.740
free in 1851.
Lady Franklin was greatly buoyed when she

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:11.481
received news that the winter camp had been
founds. But she was crushed when the search

00:12:11.481 --> 00:12:16.170
party was aborted due to an early autumn freeze.
She now directly contacted a Hudson Officer

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:20.970
named William Kennedy, who agreed to mount
a search with Lieutenant Joseph René Bellot

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:26.000
of the French navy as second-in-command. This
expedition set out in the summer of 1851 but

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.020
found no traces of either the ships or any
more camps.

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:34.190
Various searches continued through the spring
and summer of 1851. As well as sending up

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:38.990
message balloons, arctic foxes were caught
and then releases with rescue messages attached

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:45.340
to their collars. It was all to no avail and
every ship returned without success.

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:52.650
A Wife’s Will
It was thanks largely to the constant lobbying

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:57.100
of Lady Jane Franklin that the Admiralty continued
to mount the search after six years. Lady

00:12:57.100 --> 00:13:01.261
Jane also stirred up public sympathy for the
plight of her husband and his crew. The Admiralty

00:13:01.261 --> 00:13:05.710
agreed to send out a final search expedition
in the spring of 1852. It was to be commanded

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:10.760
by a Captain Belcher and consist of the Resolute
and the Assistance, along with the steamers

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:15.940
Intrepid and Pioneer. Assistance and Pioneer
headed north up Wellington Channel while the

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:21.640
Resolute and Intrepid headed west. A supply
ship, the North Star, waited at Beechey Island.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.930
Meanwhile, the HMS Investigator, which had
set out in 1850, had not returned. Then, in

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:31.100
April, 1853 an overland party from the HMS
Resolute crossed Melville Sound and came across

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:35.910
the Investigator trapped in the ice in Mercy
Bay on the north coast of Banks Island. The

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:40.380
men on board were starving to death. The rescue
arrived just as the Captain was about to send

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:45.779
out a desperate party on a one-thousand-mile
trek to a Hudson’s Bay Post on the Mackenzie

00:13:45.779 --> 00:13:50.650
River in search of food.
Finally, in 1854, a Scottish Explorer by the

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:55.370
name of John Rae discovered the fate of Franklin
and his men. Rae was surveying the Boothia

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:59.350
Peninsula on behalf of the Hudson’s Bay
Company. It was there that he was given the

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:04.210
story of the explorers by Inuit hunters. They
story was that the ships had become stuck

00:14:04.210 --> 00:14:08.750
in the ice. The men on board couldn’t get
free and so headed out on foot to find a way

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:14.180
out. They had fallen victim to either starvation
or the cold. The Indians also told Rae that

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:18.850
some of the men had given in to cannibalism.
Then, in 1855, another Hudson’s Bay man

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:22.670
by the name of James Anderson cam across one
of the boats from the Terror at the mouth

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:26.900
of the Great Fish river. Some Inuit then showed
him items that could have only come from the

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:28.410
Terror and the Erebus.

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:33.600
In January, 1854 the Admiralty declared that
the members of the expedition were to be assumed

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:38.490
dead. Their wages would be paid to their relatives
up until March 31st of that year. Lady Franklin

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:42.880
would not accept this finding. But, after
so long, she was now out on a limb. The London

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:46.279
Times editor wrote that to continue the search
would be . . .

00:14:46.279 --> 00:14:49.380
wasting time upon a search for dead men’s
bones.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:53.700
It was now up to Jane Franklin to find the
truth about her husband’s fate. She bought

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:57.790
a schooner-rigged steam yacht called the Fox
and put in command of it an experienced Arctic

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:02.920
officer named Leopold McClintock. With 25
men, McClintock set out to cross Baffin Bay

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:07.830
in the summer of 1857. However, the ship spent
the winter drifting into the ice. In early

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:13.270
1858, the Fox entered Lancaster Sound and
made its way toward Prince Regent Inlet past

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:18.920
Fury Beach to a point near Bellot Strait.
During sledging operations in early 1859,

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:23.440
McClintock came across a group of Inuit who
had items that must have belonged to the missing

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.950
men, including snow knives. Then in a village
not far from Matty Island, he purchased silver

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:33.130
spoons and forks that were engraved with Franklin’s
initials. The Inuit told him of a wreck that

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:36.880
was four day’s journey away.
McClintock and his men headed to the south

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.650
of the island. There they found the body of
a man dressed in a steward’s uniform. Then

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:45.820
a 28-foot boat was found, mounted on a sledge.
The boat was laden down with clothing and

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:51.520
other gear, including 20 kg of chocolate.
There were also two skeletons onboard.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:58.220
The Fate Revealed
At the northern tip of King William Island,

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:02.490
the searchers found a campsite, which they
named Camp Felix. Half way between the boat

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:06.241
and the camp, two piles of stones were found,
one to the north and the other to the south

00:16:06.241 --> 00:16:11.040
of a bay. Here were found single page records
of what had happened to the crew.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:14.960
The notes were both written by James Fitz
James in May, 1847.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:16.400
One of them read as follows . . .

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:24.230
April 25 1848 — H M Ships Terror and Erebus
were deserted on the 22nd April, 5 leagues

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:30.339
NNW of this, having been beset since 12th
September 1846. The officers and crews, consisting

00:16:30.339 --> 00:16:37.830
of 105 souls, under the command of Captain
F.R.M. Crozier, landed here in Lat. 69° 37’

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:44.150
42” N, Long. 98° 41’ W. This paper was
found by Lt. Irving under the cairn supposed

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:49.220
to have been built by Sir James Ross in 1831,
4 miles to the northward, where it had been

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:55.279
deposited by the late Commander Gore in June
1847. Sir James Ross’ pillar has not however

00:16:55.279 --> 00:17:00.540
been found, and the paper has been transferred
to this position, which is that in which Sir

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:05.659
J. Ross’ pillar was erected. Sir John Franklin
died on the 11th June 1847 and the total loss

00:17:05.659 --> 00:17:10.990
by deaths in the expedition has been to this
date 9 officers and 15 men.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:14.459
The Fox sailed back to England, having at
least discovered the fate of the husband of

00:17:14.459 --> 00:17:18.049
their patron. When the items that they brought
back from the condemned crew were displayed

00:17:18.049 --> 00:17:22.539
in Whitehall, it aroused great interest.
Leopold McClintock received a knighthood for

00:17:22.539 --> 00:17:26.179
his efforts in the search for Franklin and
his men. Yet it would take more than a century

00:17:26.179 --> 00:17:30.930
and half before the two ships were finally
found. In 2008 the Erebus was found. Eight

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:35.220
years later the Terror was rediscovered to
the southwest of King William Island. The

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:51.340
ship was in pristine condition.

